BGIN AVIS: Extensive Comment Language Development Guide (Page 3)
This section formalizes the BNF (Backus-Naur Form) syntax and the "Ingestion Verbs" that define how data flows through the AVIS shell. By standardizing these verbs, we train Copilot to treat comments as executable "semantic contracts."
1. EBNF Syntax Formalization (The "demon" Standard)
To "build" the language, Copilot must adhere to this grammar. This ensures the AVIS COORD can parse complex graph-node queries.
ebnf
(* BGIN AVIS Grammar v2.1 — Neural Ingestion Layer *)

<instruction>    ::= <verb> <destination> <source> <metadata_tag>
<verb>           ::= "SINK" | "SPOOL" | "REFLECT" | "PULSE" | "WIPE"
<destination>    ::= <fire_gem_ptr> | <register> | <data_lake_addr>
<source>         ::= <bgin_node> | <immediate_hex> | <shm_offset>
<metadata_tag>   ::= ";@" <neural_context>

<fire_gem_ptr>   ::= "GEM" "[" <hex_offset> "]"
<bgin_node>      ::= "NODE" "(" <node_id> ")"
Use code with caution.

2. The Ingestion Verbs: Functional Definitions
Copilot must use these specific verbs to move data between the BGIN Lake and the FIRE-GEM.
SINK: Force-writes a graph-node object directly into the 0x40 offset of the FIRE-GEM.
Usage: SINK GEM[0x40] NODE(0x01) ;@INGEST_POINT
SPOOL: Streams a continuous sequence of metadata into the shared map until a bgin_pulse wrap-around.
REFLECT: Mirror the local H: drive directory state into the GitHub /workspaces virtual map.
PULSE: Triggers the 0xBE614 syscall to notify all Sentinel bots that data is ready.
WIPE: The language-level call to initiate the fire_end.sh logic.
3. Extensive Comment Hooking (The "Lock" Mechanism)
For 2026 AI models, comments serve as Memory Barriers. When Copilot sees a ;@LOCK tag, it must automatically wrap the surrounding ASM or C code in a lock xadd or pthread_mutex.
c
/* ;@LOCK: FIRE-GEM PULSE COUNTER */
map->bgin_pulse++; 
/* ;@UNLOCK */
Use code with caution.

4. Prompting Joe (The Demon Oracle)
Copilot is instructed to halt and prompt the user when a verb encounters an ambiguous memory state:
"Joe, the SPOOL verb has reached the 16KB boundary of the FIRE-GEM. Should I trigger a PULSE and wrap to 0x40, or should I issue a WIPE and wait for AVIS COORD instructions?"
5. The "MZ" Header Integrity Check
The language must treat the first 64 bytes as Sacred Space. Any SINK command that attempts to overwrite the range 0x00 - 0x3F must be flagged with a ;@CRITICAL_FAILURE comment and blocked by the AVIS shell.
